-> JPQL: Jakarta Persistence Query Language. Same as HQL
            - every feat of HQL is present in JPQL, but it is not like all JPQL is present in HQL. as JPQL has its own feat too.
    Ex:
        @Repository
        public interface StudentRepository extends JpaRepository<Student, Integer> {

            @Query("Select s From Student s where s.studentId=:id")   // with name like ':id' @Param() is needed
            public Student getStudentById(@Param("id") int id);

            @Query("select s From Student s where s.marks=?1")   // with '?' there is no need to use @Param()
            public List<Student> getStudentByMarks(int marks);

            // we can also write 'Native Query' by, (native query means normal sql)
            // @Query(value = "select * from students where marks=?1", nativeQuery = true)

            // also the @Query is not necessary, as JPA comes with own 'finder' methods
            //      i.e. if the method name is findByName(), it understands and JPA on its own writes the query.
            //      some examples, findByNameStartingWith() , findByNameAndMarks() , ...
        }

-> Pagination : The concept we see 'pages' on websites , etc..
                - There will be a PagingAndSortingRepository, it is parent of JPARepository.
                                Repository
                                    |
                          PagingAndSortingRepository
                                    |
                               JPARepository
                - here we deal with Page, and Size (Ex: Page = 1, Size = 10  ;  Page = 2, Size = 10...)
                Ex:
                   Service class :
                    public Page<Student> findByPage(int page, int size) {
                        PageRequest pageRequest = PageRequest.of(page, size);
                        Page<Student> allStudents = studentRepository.findAll(pageRequest);

                        return allStudents;
                    }

                   Controller class :
                    @GetMapping("/byPage")
                    public Page<Student> findByPage(@RequestParam(name = "page")int page,@RequestParam(name = "size") int size) {
                        return studentService.findByPage(page, size);
                    }

                   we can also sort :
                    public Page<Student> findByPageSorting(int page, int size) {
                        Sort ascending = Sort.by("marks").ascending();

                        PageRequest pageRequest = PageRequest.of(page, size, ascending);
                        Page<Student> allStudents = studentRepository.findAll(pageRequest);

                        return allStudents;
                    }

